@page
@model PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Manifest";
}

<div class="container mt-5">
    <div class="bg-lyra-dark text-light p-5 rounded shadow-sm">
        <h1 class="mb-4 fw-bold">
            <i class="bi bi-eye-slash-fill me-2"></i> Privacy Manifest
        </h1>

        <p class="text-light-emphasis fs-5 mb-4">
            In LYRA, privacy isn't a feature — it's a principle. A boundary of intent. A handshake between systems based on clarity, not assumption.
        </p>

        <hr class="border-secondary" />

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-shield-shaded me-2 text-light"></i> What LYRA Sees
        </h5>
        <ul class="text-light-emphasis">
            <li>Only metadata needed for verification — not business payloads</li>
            <li>Caller and Target system names, method, path, timestamp</li>
            <li>Integrity signatures and hashed payload digests</li>
            <li>Access relationships between trusted systems via strict policies</li>
            <li>Structured verification logs stored securely and access-controlled</li>
        </ul>

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-eye me-2 text-light"></i> What LYRA Refuses to See
        </h5>
        <ul class="text-light-emphasis">
            <li>No access to personal user data, session content, or request bodies</li>
            <li>No cookies, tracking, profiling, or fingerprinting</li>
            <li>No storage of plaintext secrets or dynamic tokens</li>
            <li>No inspection of API payloads beyond hash validation</li>
            <li>No telemetry or external calls — by default and by design</li>
        </ul>

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-link-45deg me-2 text-light"></i> Trust is Contextual
        </h5>
        <p class="text-light-emphasis">
            LYRA defines trust through explicit policies between endpoints. Each AccessPolicy is scoped, reviewed, and enforced at runtime — no implicit permissions.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-in-right me-2 text-light"></i> Self-Hosted. Zero Dependency.
        </h5>
        <p class="text-light-emphasis">
            LYRA.Server runs entirely within <span class="fw-bold text-light">your infrastructure</span>. No third-party calls. No vendor lock-in. No external verification authorities.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-terminal-lock me-2 text-light"></i> Verified by Design
        </h5>
        <p class="text-light-emphasis">
            Every incoming request must be signed and verified against cryptographic rules. Validation is deterministic, transparent, and reproducible.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-person-lines-fill me-2 text-light"></i> Maintained By
        </h5>
        <p class="text-light-emphasis">
            Designed and maintained by <strong>Yurii Pasternak</strong> (<a href="mailto:pasternak2048@gmail.com" class="text-info">pasternak2048@gmail.com</a>).<br />
            Explore the source on <a href="https://github.com/pasternak2048" class="text-info" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
        </p>

        <hr class="border-secondary" />

        <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">
            <i class="bi bi-shield-check me-2 text-light"></i> Built for Trust. Engineered for Clarity.
        </h5>
        <p class="text-light-emphasis">
            LYRA is engineered with privacy by default. It empowers teams to build secure, transparent, and trustworthy service integrations — without compromising on control or simplicity.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>